  - The I/O half is kept out of the pure function and out of the hot path: `git_added_ts` shells `git log --diff-filter=A --format=%aI -1 -- <path>` and is passed in as the `added_ts` closure by `handle_risk_epistemic`, called **only** for the never-forecast candidate set (already a short list), never once per scored file; git missing, non-zero exit, or the empty stdout git returns for an unknown path all yield `None` → dark set, unchanged behaviour.
  + The I/O half is kept out of the pure function and out of the hot path: `git_added_ts` shells `git log --diff-filter=A --format=%H%x09%aI -1 -- <path>` (one call, sha and date tab-separated) and is passed in as the `added_ts` closure by `handle_risk_epistemic`, called **only** for the never-forecast candidate set (already a short list), never once per scored file; git missing, non-zero exit, or the empty stdout git returns for an unknown path all yield `None` → dark set, unchanged behaviour. **A fourth `None`, added Day 166 the same day the too-new split landed: a shallow clone's grafted boundary commit presents every surviving file as an addition**, so `git log --diff-filter=A` on such a repo dates every pre-window file to the boundary. `git_added_ts` therefore also returns `None` when the add-commit is a graph root of a shallow repo — decided by the pure `shallow_boundary_hides_age(is_shallow, add_sha, graph_roots)` (no I/O; all three git invocations — the `log`, `rev-parse --is-shallow-repository`, and `rev-list --max-parents=0 HEAD` — stay in `git_added_ts`, and an error from either extra call degrades to `false`/empty, i.e. today's behaviour). It compares trimmed and accepts a prefix match in either direction (one command may abbreviate where the other does not) but only when **both** sides are ≥ `MIN_SHA_PREFIX = 7` chars, so a short lucky prefix cannot excuse a genuinely dark file; `is_shallow == false` always returns false, because a full clone's root commit is a real birthday. Why it matters: the existing `None → dark set` fallback was built for *unknown* age, and a grafted date is *confidently wrong* age, which walks straight past it — `MIN_FORECAST_OPPORTUNITIES = 5` would then be asking "have ≥5 snapshots been recorded since the clone boundary?", a fact about clone depth, not about the file, and old dark rooms would silently be relabelled `too young to judge` and drop out of the exact set the exploration budget is aimed at. **Stated plainly: the harness checkout *is* shallow** (measured Day 166: `git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository` → `true`, 59 commits, and `src/prompt_utils.rs` — extracted on Day 64 — reports the grafted root `2a46fb22`, dated today, as its add-commit), so in the evolve environment this check makes the too-new split **a no-op for every file older than the clone window**: those files resolve to unknown age and stay dark. That is the honest state of the instrument, not a limitation to hide — the split only discriminates for files genuinely added inside the clone window, and the alternative (deepening the clone to go get the history) was deliberately not taken, since the fix is to stop asserting an age that cannot be observed.
